      <h2>Back to Sabine and Peter</h2>
      <p>Back to that building in Neukölln. Sabine's hope is real, but it's going to take years to test, not months, and even in the best case, the actual mechanism that would lower her rent is still working its way through coalition talks, a draft law, and almost certainly a court challenge, exactly like it did five years ago. Peter's dread is real too, but it's aimed slightly at the wrong target: nobody is coming for his two-unit building under the headline policy. What he actually needs to watch is the quieter rent-ceiling legislation that wouldn't care how many units he owns. Neither of them wakes up tomorrow to a different rent number. What's actually changed is that the range of outcomes over the next few years just got a lot wider for both of them.</p>

      <h2>What to actually do about it</h2>
      <ol class="action-list">
        <li><p><strong>If you rent in Berlin, don't budget for a lower rent this year or next.</strong> Plan around your current rent holding steady through a multi-year coalition and legal-challenge window, and treat any actual reduction as a bonus if it lands.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>If you own property or you're a small landlord, understand the size threshold that separates you from the headline policy:</strong> three thousand units or more. But watch any new rent-ceiling law specifically, since that one wouldn't care how big your portfolio is.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>If you invest in German real estate from abroad, treat Berlin-specific political risk as its own line item,</strong> distinct from Munich, Frankfurt, or Hamburg, because the market is already starting to price that difference in.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Watch the actual coalition agreement expected around the end of this year, not the election-night headline.</strong> That document, not the 25.7% number, is where you'll find out which figures turn into real law and which quietly disappear the way they did last time.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <p>Want to see how any two cities stack up for your own situation? Our free <a href="/cost-of-living-calculator">US vs Germany cost-of-living calculator</a> lets you compare rent, groceries, healthcare, transport, utilities, and how much of your salary you actually take home on each side.</p>

      <h2>The cost nobody puts on a postcard</h2>
      <p>Two neighbors, one building, one election, and one number, 25.7%, that turns out to explain a lot less about what happens next than either of them thinks this morning. It's the compensation math, the rent-ceiling court fight, and the coalition negotiation still to come, working quietly underneath a headline that already moved on to the next news cycle. That's the cost nobody puts on a postcard, and right now it's sitting in a kitchen in Neukölln, whether or not anyone in the new coalition has worked out the actual numbers yet.</p>

